# tools/al/ales_stereo_flanger_witness.rish — stereo_flanger: the short modulated delay with a feedback path, carried into stereo, the same LFO and feedback on both channels, each channel reading its own live buffer — the fifth rung of the stereo modulation class and the first stereo modulation that reads its own output (ALES180).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES180.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-084450_fill-ales180-lotus-stereo-flanger.md
# Stands on: lotus/flanger.rye (ALES73 — the mono flanger run once per channel, its FlangerError reused whole and its precheck (newly factored out) reused for the up-front atomic validation), lotus/echo.rye (ALES66 — the feedback echo
# the zero-depth flanger reduces to, the echo-degenerate equality target), lotus/glide.rye (ALES70 — glide_scale, 256 = unity), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the StereoClip), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip and the one true saturate).
#
# lotus/stereo_flanger.rye flanges a stereo master: it runs ALES73's proven mono flanger on BOTH channels with the SAME LFO and feedback — a triangle of depth round centre over period samples driving a modulated fractional delay read
# off the live buffer and fed back at fb_num/fb_den. ALES178 (vibrato) and ALES179 (chorus) carried the two snapshot delay-line modulations into stereo, each channel reading a frozen copy of its own dry; the flanger does the opposite,
# reading its short modulated delay off the live buffer and feeding the delayed voice back into itself, the moving comb (the jet-plane whoosh). That feedback returns a fault the snapshot rungs never had, BadFeedback (a zero denominator
# or a feedback at or above unity, a runaway). The same LFO and feedback on both channels, each on its own live buffer, is the linked stereo flanger, both combs sweeping in lockstep. The parameters are scaled units and fractions the
# caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the same parameters on each channel preserve the image for free; mono flanger reads the delay off the clip buffer itself, so run once per channel the left feeds back through the
# left's buffer and the right the right's, never crossed — no cross-channel feedback, no snapshot (the delay at least one whole sample, both neighbours strictly before the write index). Feedback below unity decays and a constructive
# sum past the rail saturates once per channel; because each channel's feedback loop stays wholly within its own buffer, an identical-channel (centred) master stays identical. Honestly noted: unlike the snapshot rungs the flanger does
# NOT preserve an exact panned integer ratio, the feedback's @divTrunc truncation drifting it by ones — expected of an accumulating comb, so the image law proven is the identical-channel one, the sense a mix cares about most. It
# validates the LFO, feedback, and span once against the shared length before either channel is mutated (BadPeriod, BadDepth, BadDelay, BadFeedback, BadRange), so a refusal never flanges one channel and leaves the other torn;
# FlangerError reused whole. Values only, never a length; the two channels leave balanced. The crux is five laws: the stereo flanger law (each channel equal to ALES73's mono flanger with the same LFO and feedback byte for byte, the
# channels genuinely differing), the dry-identity law (a zero-feedback flanger the dry master on both), the echo-degenerate law (a zero-depth flanger ALES66's echo at its centre on both), the identical-channel image law (an
# identical-channel master staying identical), and the balance / silence / atomicity / degenerate law.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_stereo_flanger_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-flanger: Language EN — stereo_flanger flanges a stereo master by running ALES73's proven mono flanger on both channels with the same LFO and feedback: a triangle of depth round centre over period samples driving a modulated fractional delay read off the live buffer and fed back at fb_num over fb_den. ALES178 the vibrato and ALES179 the chorus carried the two snapshot delay-line modulations into stereo, each channel reading a frozen copy of its own dry; the flanger does the opposite, reading its short modulated delay off the live buffer and feeding the delayed voice back into itself, the moving comb, the jet-plane whoosh. That feedback returns a fault the snapshot rungs never had, BadFeedback, a zero denominator or a feedback at or above unity, a runaway. The same LFO and feedback on both channels, each on its own live buffer, is the linked stereo flanger, both combs sweeping in lockstep. Mono flanger reads the delay off the clip buffer itself, so run once per channel the left feeds back through the left's buffer and the right the right's, never crossed, no cross-channel feedback, no snapshot, the delay at least one whole sample so both neighbours sit strictly before the write index. Feedback below unity decays and a constructive sum past the rail saturates once per channel; because each channel's feedback loop stays wholly within its own buffer, an identical-channel centred master stays identical. Honestly noted, unlike the snapshot rungs the flanger does not preserve an exact panned integer ratio, the feedback's truncation drifting it by ones, expected of an accumulating comb, so the image law proven is the identical-channel one. It validates the LFO, feedback, and span once against the shared length before either channel is mutated, so a refusal never flanges one channel and leaves the other torn; it writes values only, never a length, so the two channels leave balanced."
say "ales-stereo-flanger: Style Radiant — the flanger is the delay-line family's first rung that reads its short modulated delay off the live buffer and feeds the delayed voice back into itself, the moving comb whose notches slide up and down and deepen with the feedback, the jet-plane whoosh a keeper reaches for by name. ALES178 and ALES179 carried the two snapshot rungs, the vibrato and its parent the chorus, into stereo, each channel reading a frozen copy of its own dry so a moving read never reads a sample it just wrote; the flanger does the opposite, and carrying it into stereo completes the delay-line trio and is the harder crux precisely because each channel now reads its own output rather than a frozen copy. That feedback returns a fault the snapshot rungs never had, BadFeedback, a runaway that can never begin. This rung runs ALES73's proven flanger on each channel with the same LFO and feedback, so both combs sweep in lockstep and the image is held; each channel reads and feeds back through its own live buffer, the left through the left's and the right through the right's, never crossed, so there is no cross-channel feedback and no snapshot at all, because the delay is at least one whole sample so both interpolation neighbours sit strictly before the write index. Feedback strictly below unity decays, each echo smaller than the last, and a constructive sum past the rail saturates once per channel. Because each channel's feedback loop stays wholly within its own buffer, an identical-channel centred master stays identical, the image law the sense a mix cares about most; and honestly, unlike the snapshot rungs the flanger does not preserve an exact panned integer ratio, since the feedback path accumulates truncation and a right exactly half the left drifts from that ratio by ones once the loop has fed a few samples, which is expected of an accumulating feedback comb rather than a linear scaler. The crux is five laws: the stereo flanger law, each channel equal to ALES73's mono flanger with the same LFO and feedback byte for byte with the two channels genuinely differing so no channel is crossed; the dry-identity law, a zero-feedback flanger equal to the dry master on both channels so the feedback genuinely governs the comb; the echo-degenerate law, a zero-depth flanger equal to ALES66's echo at its centre on both channels so the flanger with its motion stilled is a plain feedback echo; the identical-channel image law, an identical-channel master staying identical; and the balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate law, an all-silent master flanging to silence on both with the lengths held, a bad period, a bad depth, a bad delay top, a bad feedback, and an out-of-range span each refusing by name with both channels untouched and balanced, and count zero the identity on both."
say "ales-stereo-flanger: Lens TAME — two bounded in-process i16 clips (left, right), each rung through ALES73's own live-buffer feedback delay, the triangle and the fractional interpolation run in i64 and each channel reading and feeding back through its own buffer, the fed sum saturating once; the two channels enter balanced (the StereoClip invariant asserted on entry) and leave balanced (a flanger writes values only, each length asserted equal and within bound on exit); the LFO, feedback, and span are validated once against the shared length before either channel is mutated (ALES73's precheck, factored out of the mono flanger and shared, mirroring the ALES71 chorus, ALES72 vibrato, and ALES75 ring modulator refactors), so a refusal never flanges one channel and leaves the other torn; StereoFlangerError is ALES73's FlangerError (BadPeriod, BadDepth, BadDelay, BadFeedback, BadRange) reused whole, the stereo lift adding no fault and BadFeedback being the one the snapshot rungs never had, returned by the feedback path. The stereo flanger law is proven by running both stereo_flanger and ALES73's mono flanger on the same per-channel content and demanding byte-for-byte equality on both channels while the two channels genuinely differ; the dry-identity law proves a zero-feedback flanger equals the dry master on both; the echo-degenerate law proves a zero-depth flanger equals ALES66's echo at its centre on both, an impulse on each channel exercising the whole decaying tail; the identical-channel image law proves a centred master stays identical, with the honest note that the accumulating feedback comb does not preserve an exact panned integer ratio (the @divTrunc truncation drifting it by ones); and the balance, silence, atomicity, and degenerate cases prove an all-silent master staying silent on both, the length held, each of the five refusals leaving both channels untouched and balanced, and an empty span the identity on both; siloed to lotus/, the LFO in scaled units rather than hertz and the feedback a named fraction below unity rather than a decibel, no cross-channel feedback, no snapshot, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_flanger"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_flanger.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-flanger: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_flanger.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-flanger: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-flanger: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-flanger' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-flanger: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-flanger line"
